# Manually read and curated c9 folders01–02, 2026-11-19.
C('9-001',[2,3,4],'J156-9-01','9-75;110-173','Diagramme des principaux types d’ateliers (ou de contrées)','The dated overview (15 July 1986) proposes a lattice of classes: general G; spatial S; faithful F; local L; point-like FL; set-theoretic FLS; moderate M; maquettes Mq; discrete D; empty and one-object cases. It asserts eight displayed intersection squares and, from them, LS∩M=EM. The proposed LS=S∩Div and the requirement that moderate objects be spatial and faithful remain explicitly questioned; the diagram cannot be used as an established theorem of all inclusions. The printed legend SF=spatiaux locaux conflicts with its label and must be checked against the scan.','open on the pages','ateliers')
C('9-002',[2,3],'J156-9-01','37-64','Σ ... modulaire ... donc une algèbre de Boole; À vérifier','He proposes that the complete support ortholattice satisfy arbitrary distributivity A∧∨B_i=∨(A∧B_i), calling it modular; this is stronger than the usual modular law and would indeed make the ortholattice Boolean. Whether this condition or the intended moderation implies faithfulness is questioned. He separately asks whether the support of each lieu is an atom and whether saturation makes sense for all spatial ateliers. Neither is proved in these preliminary sheets.','open on the pages','ortholattice')
C('9-003',[5,6,7,8],'J156-9-01','180-399','M0=L; M1=Drap2(L); X|°|Y ssi X<Y ou Y<X','For a connected poset L with at least two elements, form point strata P_a and interval strata I_ab for a<b; faces of I_ab are its endpoints. Open refinement is P_x≪°I_ab iff a<x<b, I_ab≪°I_cd iff c≤a<b≤d, and equality on points. Define strict separation X<Y by ex(X)≤or(Y) with X≠Y; point-point case uses the original strict order. Interior disjointness is comparability for this separation order. The text verifies the magasin axioms, then proves compatibility is equality or disjointness; therefore finite prefigures are finite chains in the separation order, and figures are those chains closed under faces. This repeats and organizes VIII scan87ff; “maquette” in the opening is not the rigid-maquette condition for arbitrary L.','proved there','intervalposets')
C('9-004',[6],'J156-9-01','319-334','X<Y ... X′≤Y′ ... exception extrémité=origine','If X<Y in the interval magasin and X′≪X, Y′≪Y, then X′≤Y′ in separation order, with equality possible only when both equal the common endpoint ex(X)=or(Y). If both refinements are open, equality is impossible. This gives hereditary interior disjointness.','proved there','intervalposets')
C('9-005',[8,9,10,11],'J156-9-01','401-430','intervalles infinis I<a,I>a ... symboles graphiques','He adjoins formal half-interval strata I_<a when a is not minimal and I_>a when a is not maximal, each with sole face a, to encode complements without adding artificial endpoints. Their naive supports are ↓a and ↑a, while distinct formal strata may have the same naive support in bounded posets. The separation order extends by declaring half-intervals outward extreme and using endpoint comparison; disjointness remains strict comparability. The proposed open-refinement formula in scan10 contains a boundary-equality conflict and is not safe to treat as a consistent axiom system without checking the ink.','stated without proof','intervalposets',confidence='low')
claims[-1]['lines'].append('J156-9-02:5-107')
C('9-006',[12],'J156-9-02','119-141','M est très dense dans M̂; ΣM̂≃ΣM','For the proposed half-interval extension, disjointness of X,Y in M̂ is detected by all original strata X′,Y′∈M with X′≪°X,Y′≪°Y. A proof is supplied for a left half-interval using an interval ending at its boundary and irreflexivity. The corollary identifies complete support ortholattices by restriction. This relies on a coherent reading of the open-refinement formulas and asserts sufficiency although the literal lemma says only “il faut”.','proof sketched','ortholattice')
C('9-007',[13,14,15],'J156-9-02','148-255','polygone complémentaire Φ*=Φ̂∖Φ; effacement des sommets redondants','For a nonempty finite chain Φ of generalized strata with vertex flag T=δΦ, the largest chain with vertex flag T consists of alternating vertices and consecutive intervals, with available outward half-intervals. Define Φ* by complement in that chain. The text claims δΦ*=T minus redundant included vertices of degree two, and Φ** is obtained by erasing such vertices and composing adjacent intervals. Empty complements require an extra convention: the prescribed construction for nonempty flags alone does not define a whole-line complement of the empty polygon. The development is restarted on scan17.','proof sketched','intervalposets')
C('9-008',[16],'J156-9-02','263-288','Th ... cosψ°M(Φ)=Omb°M(Φ*)','For a nonempty generalized polygon Φ, the announced key theorem identifies its perpendicular in the original magasin with the open shadow of its complementary polygon. A previously written endpoint hypothesis is deleted in the theorem, but Corollary1 still explicitly retains Hyp (each minimal element is least and each maximal element is greatest) when claiming the double-polar support equals Omb°M(Φ**). No proof is supplied here; Corollary2 is interrupted. These changes and the empty-complement case must be retained.','stated without proof','intervalposets')
C('9-009',[17],'J156-9-02','299-320','segment ordonné ... ω0<ω1 ... indivisibles','An ordered combinatorial segment is a poset I with distinct least and greatest elements ω0<ω1. Its interior is I minus the two endpoints. The two-element case yields the indivisible oriented one-simplex maquette; complement calculations can then stay among ordinary points and bounded intervals. The general complement calculus is restarted from this bounded model on 6 July.','definition/construction','intervalposets')
P('9-01','Boolean support lattices, moderation and faithfulness',[{'cote':'156-9','scan':3,'file':'J156-9-01','lines':'37-64'},{'cote':'156-9','scan':4,'file':'J156-9-01','lines':'134-168'}],'Does the proposed modular/Boolean condition imply faithfulness? Does the correct moderate notion imply spatiality and faithfulness? Is LS=S∩Div?','Separate three implications and use the final definitions later in IX. Arbitrary distributivity of an ortholattice is a standard Boolean condition, but no implication to faithfulness follows merely from calling Σ Boolean.')
P('9-02','Atomic supports of lieux and spatial saturation',[{'cote':'156-9','scan':3,'file':'J156-9-01','lines':'51-64'}],'For x a lieu, is supp°x an atom? Can saturation be done in the category of spatial ateliers?','For each nonzero support below supp°x, seek equality; distinguish this support-atomicity assertion from injectivity on objects. Specify the saturation construction and its universal property before testing preservation of spatiality.')
P('9-03','Complete the half-interval complement calculus',[{'cote':'156-9','scan':16,'file':'J156-9-02','lines':'263-288'}],'Key support/complement theorem announced; hypotheses altered and next corollary interrupted.','Give a consistent generalized-stratum definition, including empty and whole objects, then prove perpendicular and double-perpendicular formulas with exact endpoint hypotheses. Compare the restarted bounded-segment treatment beginning scan17 before treating this as still open.')
